```c
if (cifs_sb->root) {
    // dput(cfid->dentry);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the function `cifs_kill_sb` is called during both successful and failed mount attempts. If the mount fails, `cifs_sb->root` may not be populated, leading to the premature release of cached dentries. This can result in dereferencing invalid pointers and potential memory corruption since the cached dentries are being released even when the filesystem is not fully mounted.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (cifs_sb->root) {
    // Release cached dentries only if mount is complete
    dput(cifs_sb->root);
    cifs_sb->root = NULL;
}
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that cached dentries are only released when the filesystem mount is confirmed to be complete, indicated by the presence of a valid `cifs_sb->root`. This prevents the premature release of cached dentries during mount failures, thereby avoiding dereferencing invalid pointers and potential memory corruption.

